    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Despite its name, Boston likely originated in France during the American Revolutionary War (circa 1770s), named in honor of the siege of Boston. It was a favorite of French naval officers and rapidly spread to the United States and across Europe.',
      originCountries: [
        'France',
        'United States',
      ],
      timeline: [
        "1770s: Boston emerges in French salons as a 'revolutionary' Whist variant.",
        "1800s: Becomes extremely popular in the United States and Russia (featured in Tolstoy's 'War and Peace').",
        '1900s: Superseded by Auction Bridge but survives in traditional clubs.',
        'Present: A rare specialty game played by card game historians and historical reenactors.',
      ],
      evolution: "Boston was the first major game to introduce a full hierarchy of contracts (bids), a concept that would later become the core of Bridge. It also introduced the idea of 'Misère' bids into the Whist family.",
      cultural: 'Portrayed in 19th-century literature as the game of the intellectual and social elite. Its complexity reflected the high-stakes diplomacy of the era.',
    },
    setupContent: {
      players: '4 players playing individually (cut-throat style).',
      deck: 'Standard 52-card French pack. Two packs are often used: one for play and one for determining trump rotation.',
