          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win tricks according to the level of the declared contract. Higher contracts pay more but carry greater penalties.',
          gameplay: "1. **Auction**: Players bid for contracts (e.g., 'Boston' for 8 tricks, 'Petit Misère' for zero). 2. **Trump**: The suit of the turned-up card from the second deck usually determines the 'preference' suit for trumps. 3. **The Play**: Standard trick-taking rules. Must follow suit. 4. **Tricks**: Highest trump wins; else highest card of led suit wins.",
          keyRules: [
            "Preference Suit: One suit (usually Diamonds) is the 'Preference' and outranks other suits in the auction.",
            'Misère: A contract to lose every trick; there are no trumps in Misère hands.',
            'Independence: Unlike Whist, you usually play alone against the other three defenders.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Evaluate your hand for both high-trick potential and low-trick potential. A hand with many 7s and 8s is perfect for a Misère bid.',
          intermediate: "Auction Tactics: Use the 'Preference' suit to overcall opponents even if your trick count is lower. Knowing when to 'Double' an opponent's contract is key to defensive scoring.",
          advanced: 'Defensive Coordination: Since three players defend against one declarer, they must cooperate to force the declarer to win (in Misère) or lose (in Boston) tricks. Lead through the declarer to trap their high cards.',
          tips: [
            "Don't bid Boston (8 tricks) without at least four solid trumps and an Ace in a side suit.",
            "Watch the 'Independence' bids carefully—they are the highest-scoring and highest-risk plays.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
